ion which was neither relieved with Arginine\nnor resting. His examination was unremarkable apart from hypertension (blood pressure was 160/90) and\nbilateral basal crepitations. On 15/1/2007, his condition deteriorated. He came complaining of progressive\ndyspnea and orthopnea .His examination revealed lower limb edema up to the knees in addition to chest\ncrepitations extending to the middle lung zones. Consequently, Lasix dose was increased to 80 mg.\n\nComment [teacher1]: worsening\n\nComment [teacher2]: a\n\nComment [teacher3]: arginine and\n\nToday, the patient presented with a history of angina episode on mild exertion which lasted for 10 minutes\nyesterday. However, his complaint of dyspnea decreased. ECG confirmed anterolateral myocardial\nischemic changes.\n\nIn view of the above, I would appreciate if you provide an assessment of Mr Zu and advice regarding\ntreatment and management his condition as you see suitable. Should you have any queries, please do not\nhesitate to contact me.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nDoctor\n\nReport\n\nWord length\nComments\n\nEstimated Grade\nAdvice\n\nHelpful links\n\n194\nThis is a well written letter but some case\ninterpretation errors are visible.\nB+\n1. Revise grammar.\n2. Be careful with capitalisation.\n3. Read case notes carefully.\n4. Always proofread the letter after finishing it.\nhttps://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-reading-\npractice-tests\nhttps://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-writing-\ncorrection\nhttps://www.facebook.com/groups/oethelp\n\n296 Henley Beach Rd, Underdale, 5032\nhttp://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-writing-correction\nwww.benchmarkedu.com.au/pteoetielts\n\nComment [teacher4]: as well as the\n\nComment [teacher5]: of", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 841.92, "height": 1190.64, "blocks": [{"text": "Ticket Id: 25703", "lines": [{"text": "Ticket Id: 25703", "x": 0.5240046560243254, "y": 0.049343210374252496, "width": 0.09857444887875327, "height": 0.010784116105623869, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "T